  "query_preview": "..A warming trend will continue as high pressure\nremains overhead through the first part of the weekend. A cut off\nupper level low currently over southern California will continue\nto retrograde westward. Meanwhile, the continued very active\neastern Pacific will have at least two ongoing tropical cyclones\n(Simon and Trudy). As the upper level low continues to shift\nwestward, tropical moisture from weakening Tropical Storm Simon\nwill be drawn northward into Baja California and eventually the\nDesert Southwest Sunday. \n\nThis upper level low will eject across the Desert Southwest ahead\nof the next Pacific trough Sunday. PW anomalies from this \nmoisture advection ahead of the low will reach near 90-95th \npercentile across southern Utah. While it is mid-October,\nif sufficient destabilization occurs, a few thunderstorms capable\nof heavy rain will develop Sunday afternoon and evening across\nsouthern Utah. This threat will continue Monday afternoon and\nevening.\n\nThe previously mentioned upper level trough will be off the\ncentral California Coast by Tuesday morning. Broad upper level\ndivergence ahead of this upper level trough combined with PVA \nwill support the develop of widespread precipitation across \ncentral and southern Utah by Tuesday afternoon. As this trough \nslowly crosses the West, additional periods of valley rain and \nhigh elevation snow will continue through at least the early \nweekend period. Rainfall totals (25th to 75th percentile) are \naveraging around 0.50 to 1.50 inches across most locations, with \nsome areas that do well in southerly flow (northern Wasatch Front,\nProvo Canyon, southern mountains) likely seeing more than 2 \ninches. As far as snow, while it's over several days, looking like\nanother 6 to 12 inches, mainly above 9000-9500 feet. \n\nGoing Freeze Warnings are on track and no changes will be made. No\nadditional headlines are needed at this juncture.",
